@Gradient
The gradient menu can be used to make a gradient fill. There are two ways
to be used:

þ Mark the tiles that you want to fill, and then push v and select the
rectangular area (to use as the default end points) and then push g.

þ Mark any tiles to exclude from the fill (optional), and then push v and
select the rectangular area to be filled and then push G.

This menu has three submenus:
!s;Shape
!p;Pattern
!o;Option

:s;Shape

The options in this menu will be used to decide a number which is then used
as the pattern offset. The range is normally 0 to 1.

You must specify the coordinates of the end points for the gradient; by
default, the rectangular area will be specified. If you select "Reverse"
then the two end points are exchanged, which effectively reverses the
order of the pattern (although this does not affect the "Integer" shape).

You must also select one of the following types of shapes:

þ Linear gradient: Makes a linear gradient from the first end point to the
second end point.

þ Circular gradient: Makes a circular gradient according to the angle,
starting from the first end point. The first direction will be toward
the second end point.

þ Euclidean radius: Uses the Euclidean distance from one end point.

þ Chebyshev radius: Uses the Chebyshev distance from one end point.

þ Manhattan radius: Uses the Manhattan distance from one end point.

þ Distance from line: Uses the distance from the point to the line
specified by the two end points.

þ Distance from points: Based on the sum of the distance from the point
to each of the two end points.

þ Integer: Uses integer arithmetic only. Multiplies the X coordinate by the
X factor and the Y coordinate by the Y factor and is modulo the number of
pieces of the pattern.

þ Random: Ignores coordinates entirely and uses the pieces of the selected
pattern at random.

The following additional options are available (which ones are available
depends on the type of shape):

þ X/Y factors: Some shapes use these as multipliers for the X and Y
coordinates of the tile when deciding the pattern offset.

þ Divisor: This number will divide the generated number in order to
effectively stretch the pattern.

:p;Pattern

The following types of patterns are available:

þ Current color: Uses the current color and kind with a fixed pattern of
characters (which is displayed in the preview area).

þ Current color with string: Like above but you can specify your own
sequence of characters to use.

þ Preset: You can select one of the built-in pattern sequences of colours
and characters. The current kind is used.

þ Block area: Uses the tiles in the block area as the pattern. (This is
not affected by changes to the shape coordinates; it will always use the
originally selected block.)

You can specify the start/end offsets, which will truncate the pattern at
the start or end by removing that many pieces. (You can see the effect of
this in the preview area.)

:o;Option

You can select the layer to apply the gradient to. (It will automatically
select the main layer or over layer if that is the layer that is currently
being edited, but you can change this.)

You can select what properties of the tiles should be affected, which can
be the kind, colour, and/or parameter. See the above section about patterns
for how each of these is decided.

For stats, you can select whether to clear stats in the gradient area or
to avoid overwriting stats. If you select both, then any stats will be
cleared but will otherwise remain unchanged. If you do not clear the stats,
then the tiles will continue to belong to the stats that they already are.

